Relevant columns & how they will be used  

actor.actor_id          : joins an actor to the films she/he played in  
actor.first_name         : first part of the actor’s full name (for the answer)  
actor.last_name          : second part of the actor’s full name (for the answer)  
film.film_id             : “glue” column that lets us tie a film to categories, actors, etc.  
film.language_id         : links to language.language_id so we can keep only English‑language films  
film.length              : running time – must be ≤ 120 minutes  
film.rating              : must be either ‘G’ or ‘PG’  
film.release_year        : must be between 2000 and 2010 (inclusive)  
film_category.category_id: links a film to one or more categories  
category.name            : we keep only rows whose name = 'Children'  
film_actor.actor_id      : associates films and actors (what we need for the frequency count)  
language.name            : language of the film – should be ‘English’  

Main filter conditions  

language.name          = 'English'  
category.name          = 'Children'  
film.rating            IN ('G','PG')  
film.length            <= 120  
film.release_year      BETWEEN '2000' AND '2010'   -- release_year is VARCHAR(4) in Sakila  

Below are ten exploratory‑to‑final SQLite queries (each limited to 20 rows where applicable).  
Run them in sequence to understand the data and finally obtain the desired actor.  

```sql
-- 1) Show all language names that exist
SELECT DISTINCT name
FROM language
LIMIT 20;

-- 2) Show all category names that exist
SELECT DISTINCT name
FROM category
LIMIT 20;

-- 3) Take a quick look at the rating values that occur in the film table
SELECT DISTINCT rating
FROM film
LIMIT 20;

-- 4) List films (id, title) that are English‑language
SELECT DISTINCT f.film_id, f.title
FROM film AS f
JOIN language AS l ON f.language_id = l.language_id
WHERE l.name = 'English'
LIMIT 20;

-- 5) Narrow those English films to the Children category
SELECT DISTINCT f.film_id, f.title
FROM film              AS f
JOIN language          AS l  ON f.language_id       = l.language_id
JOIN film_category     AS fc ON f.film_id           = fc.film_id
JOIN category          AS c  ON fc.category_id      = c.category_id
WHERE l.name = 'English'
  AND c.name = 'Children'
LIMIT 20;

-- 6) Apply the remaining film‑level filters (year, length, rating)
SELECT DISTINCT f.film_id, f.title
FROM film              AS f
JOIN language          AS l  ON f.language_id  = l.language_id
JOIN film_category     AS fc ON f.film_id      = fc.film_id
JOIN category          AS c  ON fc.category_id = c.category_id
WHERE l.name       = 'English'
  AND c.name       = 'Children'
  AND f.rating     IN ('G','PG')
  AND f.length     <= 120
  AND f.release_year BETWEEN '2000' AND '2010'
LIMIT 20;

-- 7) For those filtered films, list the actors who took part
SELECT DISTINCT fa.actor_id, a.first_name, a.last_name
FROM film              AS f
JOIN language          AS l  ON f.language_id  = l.language_id
JOIN film_category     AS fc ON f.film_id      = fc.film_id
JOIN category          AS c  ON fc.category_id = c.category_id
JOIN film_actor        AS fa ON f.film_id      = fa.film_id
JOIN actor             AS a  ON fa.actor_id    = a.actor_id
WHERE l.name       = 'English'
  AND c.name       = 'Children'
  AND f.rating     IN ('G','PG')
  AND f.length     <= 120
  AND f.release_year BETWEEN '2000' AND '2010'
LIMIT 20;

-- 8) Count how many of those qualifying films each actor appeared in
SELECT a.actor_id,
       a.first_name,
       a.last_name,
       COUNT(*) AS film_count
FROM film              AS f
JOIN language          AS l  ON f.language_id  = l.language_id
JOIN film_category     AS fc ON f.film_id      = fc.film_id
JOIN category          AS c  ON fc.category_id = c.category_id
JOIN film_actor        AS fa ON f.film_id      = fa.film_id
JOIN actor             AS a  ON fa.actor_id    = a.actor_id
WHERE l.name       = 'English'
  AND c.name       = 'Children'
  AND f.rating     IN ('G','PG')
  AND f.length     <= 120
  AND f.release_year BETWEEN '2000' AND '2010'
GROUP BY a.actor_id, a.first_name, a.last_name
ORDER BY film_count DESC, a.last_name, a.first_name
LIMIT 20;

-- 9) Identify the actor(s) who have the maximum count
SELECT sub.actor_id,
       sub.first_name,
       sub.last_name,
       sub.film_count
FROM (
    SELECT a.actor_id,
           a.first_name,
           a.last_name,
           COUNT(*) AS film_count
    FROM film              AS f
    JOIN language          AS l  ON f.language_id  = l.language_id
    JOIN film_category     AS fc ON f.film_id      = fc.film_id
    JOIN category          AS c  ON fc.category_id = c.category_id
    JOIN film_actor        AS fa ON f.film_id      = fa.film_id
    JOIN actor             AS a  ON fa.actor_id    = a.actor_id
    WHERE l.name       = 'English'
      AND c.name       = 'Children'
      AND f.rating     IN ('G','PG')
      AND f.length     <= 120
      AND f.release_year BETWEEN '2000' AND '2010'
    GROUP BY a.actor_id, a.first_name, a.last_name
) AS sub
WHERE sub.film_count = (
      SELECT MAX(film_count) FROM (
          SELECT COUNT(*) AS film_count
          FROM film              AS f
          JOIN language          AS l  ON f.language_id  = l.language_id
          JOIN film_category     AS fc ON f.film_id      = fc.film_id
          JOIN category          AS c  ON fc.category_id = c.category_id
          JOIN film_actor        AS fa ON f.film_id      = fa.film_id
          WHERE l.name       = 'English'
            AND c.name       = 'Children'
            AND f.rating     IN ('G','PG')
            AND f.length     <= 120
            AND f.release_year BETWEEN '2000' AND '2010'
          GROUP BY fa.actor_id
      )
)
LIMIT 20;

-- 10) Final concise query (single row) – gives the full name of the most‑frequent actor
SELECT a.first_name || ' ' || a.last_name  AS full_name,
       COUNT(*)                            AS films_in_scope
FROM film              AS f
JOIN language          AS l  ON f.language_id  = l.language_id
JOIN film_category     AS fc ON f.film_id      = fc.film_id
JOIN category          AS c  ON fc.category_id = c.category_id
JOIN film_actor        AS fa ON f.film_id      = fa.film_id
JOIN actor             AS a  ON fa.actor_id    = a.actor_id
WHERE l.name       = 'English'
  AND c.name       = 'Children'
  AND f.rating     IN ('G','PG')
  AND f.length     <= 120
  AND f.release_year BETWEEN '2000' AND '2010'
GROUP BY a.actor_id, a.first_name, a.last_name
ORDER BY films_in_scope DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

Execute the last statement (Query 10) to obtain the actor’s full name who starred most often in English‑language Children’s films, rated G/PG, 120 min or shorter, released 2000‑2010.
